rown  deluna  begin when you know wouldn't it people  start receiving him well if just after  Harpers Ferry a lot of the Southerners  well initially they praise him but the  but then they called him crazy but the  lunatic thing really kicked in in the  1880s just after reconstruction  collapsed and I won't get the histories  in my book but basically yeah a series  of people came out and said he was crazy  and then a number of historians  including the famous Allan Nevins Allan  Nevins who was a very very famous Civil  War historian said he should have been  in a hospital for the criminally insane  that that's what he should have been and  why did that arise because during that  whole period I mentioned of segregation  and Jim Crow even historians to some  degree reflected the temper of that era  and so they demonized John Brown and  else in other extreme abolitionists and  and they called him crazy yes yeah I  mean the when John Brown came to Concord  shortly after the Pottawatomie massacre  and he described his violent methods he  didn't admit to Potawatomi to them  explicitly but the roast said that a guy  like John Brown should be made a hero of  even if he were the most vile murderer  that's what Thoreau said it was kind of  shocking to say that that's into roast  speech about John Brown and Potawatomi  was uttered was described on the floor  of the United States Senate in 1856 it  was known publicly and a lot of my  research shows that it became public  knowledge much more so than previous  biographers have realized I go back to  the newspapers of the day and I show and  the Senate reports and it was there and  even after everybody in the world knew  it because the New York Herald  shortly after Harpers Ferry said John  Brown pulled these five people out and  slaughter them at Potawatomi even after  that  actually Emerson kept referring to him  to Jesus Christ and so forth even even  in full knowledge of all of that so yeah  pert pretty amazing this was kind of